Keep the host's interrupt table while bringing a guest up.

The host installs an IDT whose every vector lands on a stub that records what
happened and hands control back, on an IST stack so even a destroyed stack
pointer is survivable. The kernel replacing it during early boot removes the
only thing able to report a fault, at exactly the point where faults are
expected -- and a fault whose handler is not ready is a double fault, then a
triple fault, which resets the machine the guest is a process inside.

Gated on co_colinux_guest, which the host sets before the guest runs, so a
kernel booted on real hardware is unaffected.

A guest that actually works will want its own traps eventually. That decision
belongs after the kernel can survive taking one.

Four of the five sites are in idt.c and none of them is the first to run.
head_64.S calls early_setup_idt about twenty-five instructions after the host
hands control over -- before start_kernel, let alone setup_arch -- and that one
load is enough to take the host's debug trap away. Single-stepping is the only
bound on a guest running with interrupts disabled, so losing it does not
produce a fault to report: the guest never comes back, the host's ioctl never
returns, and the machine stops dead with no bugcheck, no dump and no reset.
That is the difference between a diagnosis and a power button.

